import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Ecclesiastes",
  display: "Ecclesiastes",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 01"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you like simple, down to earth, easily understood, fluffy rainbows and unicorn books of the Bible, you are certainly not going to find it here. Honestly, you probably won't find it much of the Bible. Every way you look in scripture there is some sort of issue that faithful men face. If it's not Jacob facing the daunting task of pleasing his wives, or his children literally trying to kill on of his sons, or David facing battles, and fleshly lusts with Bathsheba (Solomon's (the supposed author of this book) mother), or you go ahead and pick your character, Solomon has an interesting approach to this book of the Bible as far as setting the tone."},{"kind":"para","text":"We will have to establish a few things while we read. First, we should establish who the author is. The author never downright says who he is in any of the chapters here. The conclusion that most scholars have come to is that it was Solomon, and I tend to agree for a few reasons. We will go through that as we read, but we can rightfully determine it's Solomon based on the amount of wisdom that is going to be poured out in these pages."},{"kind":"para","text":"Secondly, we need to establish the tone. The tone shifts up and down throughout each chapter. The author will talk about highlights of his life, the amount of work he has had to go through to get there, and what he has found some fleeting joy in for the time being while he works. He also talks (quite heavily) about how none of it actually even matters in the end anyway because it will all just be taken away or abused. His hard work will go to someone else. This, to me anyway, sets the tone for MOST (if not ALL) of God's faithful members throughout all of scripture. Including Jesus Christ, they all have some sort of difficult battle they have to face and they feel completely defeated by it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Thirdly, we should establish (by the end of this) what the ultimate purpose of this book is for. We will start off by addressing HOW we should focus this book, and where we should focus this book, but it should conclude with the answer and solution for utilizing this book of the Bible. I don't think I've ever heard but maybe one sermon from this book of the Bible. It's rich with great advice and practical easy to use thoughts for when things are looking quite bleak either in your life, or (rather especially) in your faith. We are going to walk through valleys a lot more often than standing on mountain tops. There will be mountain top times, but they won't be as numerous as the dark valley's we have to tread because this Earth (as long as we are here) is a very dark and cruel place."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Vanities of Life","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Right off the bat, we have the three main establishments we have already spoken about. The writer starts himself off by calling himself the “Preacher.” May we not forget that everything in scripture points to Jesus Christ, including this book of the Bible. I could argue that this book of the Bible is a mixture between the struggles with our own minds and wants, and the wants and mind of the Spirit in our daily walks. We will have to face struggles if we are going to follow Christ. The preacher could be the Holy Spirit referencing this to you. It’s a sobering reminder that we have a voice speaking to us from God’s Word, and it isn’t yourself that makes that voice. The Lord speaks through His Word. That is what a preacher does by the way; he speaks God’s Word. If a preacher hardly touches the word of God or doesn’t reference Christ in His sermon, I hardly think to call him a preacher at all. We will see that this preacher spoken about in this book of the Bible is very much influenced by the life and legacy of Jesus Christ and His finished work on the cross."},{"kind":"para","text":"Next, he calls himself “the son of David.” Now we could reference quite a few people as “David’s” sons. Thankfully, the Bible gives us quite a few genealogies to be sure our information is correct:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now these were the sons of David, which were born unto him in Hebron; the firstborn Amnon, of Ahinoam the Jezreelitess; the second Daniel, of Abigail the Carmelitess: The third, Absalom the son of Maachah the daughter of Talmai king of Geshur: the fourth, Adonijah the son of Haggith: The fifth, Shephatiah of Abital: the sixth, Ithream by Eglah his wife. These six were born unto him in Hebron; and there he reigned seven years and six months: and in Jerusalem he reigned thirty and three years. And these were born unto him in Jerusalem; Shimea, and Shobab, and Nathan, and Solomon, four, of Bathshua the daughter of Ammiel: Ibhar also, and Elishama, and Eliphelet, And Nogah, and Nepheg, and Japhia, And Elishama, and Eliada, and Eliphelet, nine. These were all the sons of David, beside the sons of the concubines, and Tamar their sister.","ref":"1 Chronicles 3:1-9"},{"kind":"para","text":"The last part of this verse states that his son was “kin in Jerusalem.” This phrase only applies to two sons. It could either be Absolom, who certainly wouldn’t have written this before his death, and there is no other information about the wisdom that pours out of this book of the Bible defining Absolom, or it could be Solomon. It’s a pretty obvious choice between the two. If you don’t know anything about Solomon, this book is probably not going to align historically accurately for you. It’s best to study up on Solomon in 1 Kings to get a pretty clear picture of who he was and why he needed to write this book of the Bible."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, he doesn’t want to be referenced as “Solomon” or “Peace” right now. He wants to be referenced as “the Preacher” because this needs to stick to the hearts of the hearers. This is all about Jesus Christ!"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we stop for a second, and you say, “This doesn’t sound like Jesus Christ. This sounds sad already!” Is this a message of Christ? To this very lost and calloused world, yes! We cling onto this world with everything we’ve got, but the Lord (and the Preacher, capital “P” by the way) will tell you that nothing in this world is actually valuable. It all will go away."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the world passeth away, and the lust thereof: but he that doeth the will of God abideth for ever.","ref":"1 John 2:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is something we are warned about over and over again throughout all of scripture, but we will definitely understand why through this book of scripture. Everything is vanity. “Vanity” means “vapour” or “breath.” So Solomon basically says, “Everything is just a single breath.” This sermon (to Solomon) is just a “breath of breath’s.” That is what every sermon is. It’s a breath of breath’s. People will either take in the quick breath you give out, or they will miss it. It’s not up to the preacher where to use their breath."},{"kind":"para","text":"Imagine if we chose when to breathe. We had to savor our breath’s until we get to a place that it’s useful for. We would all pass out sitting at home. But that’s what a sermon is. It’s a breath that needs to be released. It needs to come out, otherwise the preacher suffocates. I know the feeling quite well…"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"People could argue, “Well, there’s a lot of profit for our work. I work, I get paid, I use the money for bills and fun!” Honestly, if we put life into that small bubble, we could understand why this Preacher is preaching about the small breath of life. Everything is just a small breath away and then instantly gone. Your money, that you worked so hard for, goes to something else. Hardly ever does it go to yourself, and when it does, it is not enough. You need more. That just gets you into a lifeless circle of work. The Preacher will make it clear how meaningless it all seems."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Everyone knows the tune for “Circle of Life,” and we could all probably sing at least those words right now. You probably think of the Lion King while you sing it. The circle of life, to the Preacher (and that is to every Preacher, not just Solomon and not just Jesus), is just vanity. One generation comes in, passes away, the next generation comes after. It’s just one big spiral. It’s not a circle. It should read “The Spiral of Life.” We don’t finish where we started like a circle would suggest. We end up somewhere entirely different. Either we are spiraling up, or spiraling down. Arguably, every generation spirals further and further down because they drive themselves further and further away from the Lord. But I am jumping the gun here; I’ll let the Preacher preach."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The sun is dependable. It always shows itself in the morning and disappears in the evening. Life, however, is not that dependable. Imagine if the sun didn’t do that tomorrow. We would be a very confused people. Honestly, it would probably scare me if the sun didn’t show itself tomorrow."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"These are all dependable. They are “cycles” in our world. That is the scientific term for it. The sun is in a cycle. Seasons are in a cycle. Rivers (or water) are in a cycle. Wind runs in cycles. They are expected. We might not know WHEN to expect them, but it’s really just a matter of expecting it eventually."},{"kind":"para","text":"The sun is the most predictable out of all these cycles. Rain isn’t. Wind isn’t. We can’t depend on those things. God will provide them in His own timing. There are people who try to figure it out, but they never will. They are literally chasing the wind. How much more vain can you get? I think the literal definition of vanity should be “Chasing the wind.”"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We always want more. Our flesh is never satisfied. This is just honest knowledge being spewed here. A man with Christ sees the vanity of life and the pursuit of material goods. A man without God thinks that this is all life is about—chasing things that are vanity. The pursuit is vanity, and the prize is vanity. Eventually, you will run your life and time into nothing because everything is nothing in this world."},{"kind":"para","text":"Tell me something that lasts forever on this Earth. If you find something that will outlive you, is it worth the pursuit? I can think of a few things that are worth the pursuit, and none of them revolve around “labor,” per se. But we will get into that in later chapters; I don’t want to spoil anything."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have quite a few developments in our age today. Do you think that Solomon would feel the same way if he viewed our current scientific achievements? Do you think he would say, “This is not new, this is just the same thing that has already been. We simply just developed something further that someone else already did.” Think about it. Solomon is writing this in a time far before electricity and technological advancements. He has no idea that these things are going to exist. He doesn’t even know what a skyscraper is. He would probably think it’s funny. I, personally, believe he would say the same thing. He would still be the richest man in the world, and he would look at our advancements and simply say, “This is nothing new. We are developing further, but what’s it all really for in the end?” Honestly, he’s right. Nothing really seems to “advance.” It’s pushing further, but nothing is advanced. It’s like the iPhone. Cool at the time it came out, and it was different, but it was more of the same thing we had already been using in our pockets for years prior. Before that, we pushed forward from home lines, and before that it was Morse code on the radio. We certainly have developed further, but where do we go from here? Every iPhone since the first one has been the same thing with “higher-end” specs. We are just developing the same things, with different features and calling it a “breakthrough in technology.” We have already seen versions of this before for generations. How is it a breakthrough at this point?"},{"kind":"para","text":"Don’t get me wrong, I like the latest and greatest technology just as much as the next guy, but in the end, it’s all vanity really. It’s a waste of time and money because something else will come, and they will call that the next best thing, and you will have to pursue that next. This verse describes our generation to the letter. If this is what we are amazed with by our finite brains here on Earth, just imagine what the Lord has stored up for us in Heaven."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"They keep touting it as new, but it’s actually not as new as you think. Even cellphones. The government has been using cellphones for years before we had them available in our own pockets. We finally just got access to them. But further than that, Solomon is a “Preacher” at the moment, and the only thing that Preachers are concerned with developing is the heart and Spirit of a person. That is what is not new in this world."},{"kind":"para","text":"When Solomon says, “What can you call new on this Earth?” he is speaking primarily about our sinful and needful nature of life. When we boil it all down to that, nothing really is new. We are all the same when it comes to sin and pain. That is why we are able to support others through their trials, because we all have experienced them before. There is nothing new."},{"kind":"para","text":"Your children might think they are the only ones going through their emotions and pains, but it’s nothing new."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is also nothing new. Good things come, we are enamored by them for a time, and then we forget about them throughout the years. Someone else develops it later on and brings it back, and that generation thinks it’s the coolest thing until they (too) forget about it years down the line. That’s another cycle of life: forgetting what was great for something that looks greater. We are vain beings."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":18,"title":"The Preacher King","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Was” is an interesting word. I assume he is still king. Perhaps he’s writing this as an old man, and he is seeing that his kingdom is going to his son. I would believe that to be the case. He is probably worried about the progress he has made and now that he is coming to the end of his life, he’s looking back and thinking, “What did I actually do that matters?” If you have not read the accounts of Solomon, then you probably don’t know what he has done that actually matters. He did a lot for the people of Israel. There is no other time in recorded history when Israel prospered the most than under the leadership of Solomon. Yet it was not Solomon that the people were supposed to prosper under. That is why Solomon has this heavy weight on his shoulders as he’s writing this. He is realizing that the people depended too much on their king. They needed to depend on the Lord, and we will see throughout this book of scripture that Solomon is pleading with the readers to take that into consideration moving forward. The kingdom is not resting upon the shoulders of Solomon. This is for the readers today. Your household, your church, your congregation, your company, your whatever does not rest upon your shoulders. If it does, it will fall apart the moment you fall apart (or the moment you pass on). It has to rest on the shoulders of the Lord. Don’t carry the burden of leadership without the foundation of Christ. Otherwise, you have the Catholic Church. I’m kidding (slightly) of course. It’s not just the Catholic Church that has this problem; it’s any ism in this world. If you are part of a religion, the truth, protection, and continuation of that religion are hinged upon the leader’s shoulders. If the leader passes away, so does your church and religion. The current Pope of the Catholic Church has died recently, and it has been a race to replace him. Why? Because if there is no pope, there is no Catholic religion. Their faith relies on the shoulders of their Pope, and if their Pope is dead, their works are dead as well. Thankfully, Christ rose from the dead, so when we place our works, faith, desires, lives, and leadership on His shoulders, He will carry it long after we die. Solomon is just starting to realize this, and that is going to be the theme of this book. It’s a warning for anyone that takes the leadership next (which will be his son Rehoboam). Spoiler alert, his son ignores all this advice. In fact, I could argue that his son doesn’t read any of his father’s advice. It will be Rehoboam that splits and destroys the nation of Israel literally right after the prosperity of his father. The people leaned too much on Solomon’s leadership, and so the legacy of Israel died the moment that Solomon died. The legacy of salvation died the moment that Jesus died as well. But the moment Jesus rose from the dead, we have a living and obvious legacy that rests upon our leader that lives forever."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon fell for the same traps as the rest of the world. His leadership and riches didn’t keep him from making the same mistakes the poorest man would have made. He made the same mistakes you have made as well. He is simply a human being after all. Solomon says that God allows us to make these mistakes simply to be “exercised” with in this life. We learn by our mistakes. Well, maybe not all of us, but we certainly SHOULD be learning by the mistakes we have made. We will either learn what not to do, or we will learn by strenuous consequences as we continue to seek it even though it hurts us. Either way, God will not stop us from making mistakes, and He will not force us to make the right decisions. He will simply let our actions play out, and we will either have to turn to Him in genuine repentance and trust, or reject Him to make the same dumb mistakes again. I’d say that 90% of the time we will simply make the same dumb mistakes again (even if we are in the faith)."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, he is probably telling the truth. From his throne, he has likely seen everything that has been done, even the most heinous of things. We know from reading that Solomon had an idolatry problem. That is a good reason why the people under his leadership shouldn’t have depended on him so much. He was a flawed leader, even though they were extremely prosperous during his reign. He was a wise leader, but he still struggled with sin. Ask one of his four hundred wives about it…"}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are all born crooked by the way. I’ve heard some in the LGBT community say, “I was born this way.” They are right. We are all born with a sinful nature. We are all crooked, and we cannot be made straight. Perhaps it’s not homosexuality that you are dealing with in your life and walk, but there is certainly something that you have been crooked about your entire life."},{"kind":"para","text":"If a tree grows crooked, you will not make it straight. If a branch is crooked, you cannot make it straight. If a sinner is born crooked, you cannot make it straight. However, the Lord can make you a new thing. You must be born of His Spirit. His Spirit has no perverseness. It is as straight as an arrow. It accomplishes the very best of the Lord, and if we want to stop being crooked, all we must do is allow the Lord to correct us. We like to fight against that though, and so we are always a little bent out of shape."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Vexation” means “longing” or “striving,” usually in a negative way. It involves constantly pursuing something without an affirmative end. For Solomon, arguably the wisest man to have ever lived (aside from Christ), seeking after knowledge was futile. He probably knew quite a lot by the time he was an old man. He could likely tell you things about everything in this world that you didn’t know you could know, yet in the end, it really doesn’t account for anything. I want us to look at one example that Solomon gives because it perfectly encapsulates what he is saying here:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Solomon's wisdom excelled the wisdom of all the children of the east country, and all the wisdom of Egypt. For he was wiser than all men; than Ethan the Ezrahite, and Heman, and Chalcol, and Darda, the sons of Mahol: and his fame was in all nations round about. And he spake three thousand proverbs: and his songs were a thousand and five. And he spake of trees, from the cedar tree that is in Lebanon even unto the hyssop that springeth out of the wall: he spake also of beasts, and of fowl, and of creeping things, and of fishes. And there came of all people to hear the wisdom of Solomon, from all kings of the earth, which had heard of his wisdom.","ref":"1 Kings 4:30-34"},{"kind":"para","text":"Having so much wisdom to write proverbs, songs, and to know everything there is to know about trees, animals, fish, and creeping things is pretty cool. I watch people like Steve Irwin or The Outdoor Boys, and they spew out so much information about the environment and animals that I never knew about. Yet, what does all that information matter for? I’ve never been in a situation where I needed to know all of this, and even if I was, I wouldn’t remember what they had said anyway. It was cool for the moment, but then all that information just disappears."},{"kind":"para","text":"The same goes for Solomon’s Wisdom. It was useful and achieved what it was supposed to, but when he dies, all that information will go to nothing. Getting so knowledgeable and powerful doesn’t really lead to any grand prize at the end of life. You just feel empty still. That’s going to be the theme of every chapter from here until the end, so buckle up."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“The smarter I got, the more miserable life became.” This is a very stark and important warning to every reader here. It doesn’t matter how much you know; it only matters how much you use. It’s one thing to have a certificate in chemistry and yet work in fast food. Having that knowledge means nothing if you are not using it for something. There are a lot of very wise and intelligent people in this world who are squandering their wisdom because they have no outlet for it. It’s usually the world that keeps them from using their talents. So they know all this stuff, but they can’t use it, and it really just makes them depressed."},{"kind":"para","text":"To have a calling with no answer is one of the worst things a person in this life can experience. To have a church calling and yet be shunned by the church is painful. To have a calling to preach and yet have no ability to do it is depressing. To have the ability to account, perform, and love what you do and yet be blocked from doing it because of degrees or arrogant leadership is a sorrowful thing. Yet that is what most of our world is structured with. Not Solomon, though. If you look at Solomon’s resume, you would see he was the least qualified to take the throne. Furthermore, the people he put around him were not anything to look at. But they were talented. They were good at what they did, and instead of Solomon trying to find the best fit by his own standards, he put people into prominent roles based on their abilities and calling."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve never seen a leader do that today, and these corporate industries are struggling because of it. Now everyone is just a number that can be replaced if you don’t live up to their expectations. What a sad world we have built today. So many people have wisdom and knowledge and yet no way to use it. Gifts should be encouraged. Callings should be pursued (with the help of godly leaders). Wisdom should be utilized, and knowledge should be poured out to others who can do the same."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost: Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world. Amen.","ref":"Matthew 28:19-20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the things that thou hast heard of me among many witnesses, the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also.","ref":"2 Timothy 2:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, that is not the structure of any sort of leadership in our world today. Everyone wants to rob everyone else of their gifts and then shun them. That is not how it should be."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":18}]},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 02"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, the Preacher opened this up to exclaim how dismal and temporary life really is. It doesn't start as a message of hope and prosperity. It starts as a message of hurt and wastefulness. A life pursuing Earthly gain is a life well-wasted. That is a nail that Solomon will hit on the head over and over again throughout each chapter of this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter Solomon doesn't stop talking about how fleeting everything in this life really is. We get practical information about the high life, and why it leaves you still feeling empty, how eventually there is one event that everyone in this life will eventually experience, and he ends this chapter off with what bad focus leaves us with."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":11,"title":"Everything Is Nothing","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we looked in our world today for what “pleasure” is, we would probably narrow it down to a few options. Maybe it’s a drug, or perhaps some sort of show or amusement park. It could be a feeling or a situation, or even a city or town like Las Vegas. Maybe it’s a country or place, such as Babylon. Pleasure is often characterized by how much fun you get out of it. To the world, pleasure is self-seeking. To the Christian, it’s the opposite."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you go to Chick-fil-A today, the people that serve you will say, “My pleasure” every single time you say thank you. Why? Well, it’s because they are trained to say it, but the true Christian knows it really IS their pleasure to serve others. Most of the characters at Chick-fil-A are probably not Christian, and they probably don’t really care to serve you. It is not a pleasure to them; it’s a paycheck. If I can be honest, I think a lot of that same mentality has entered into the church. The leadership doesn’t really care that much about serving you. It’s not a pleasure to them. No, they get real genuine pleasure from being served, rather than from serving. They want something in return for their service. They serve with a hand out so they can get something back from you, rather than just doing it because it truly is their pleasure."},{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon tells us here that he was looking for pleasure in this world, but it was all vanity (or a breath). It didn’t last long. He probably had more pleasures at his fingertips than any other king in human history, yet it didn’t suffice his needs for more. It was never enough. It will never be enough for you either if you are just looking to try and please yourself."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Mirth” means “joy,” “gladness,” or “pleasure.” Even laughing was useless to Solomon. Eventually, what he thought was funny no longer became funny."},{"kind":"para","text":"I remember I used to know a joke that made a lot of people laugh when I was in middle school. Every time I said it, people would laugh hysterically. I would say it nearly every day for every situation because there were many times when it was useful. Until one day, I oversaid it, because a kid in front of me finally turned around and said, “Don’t you have any other material? You say the same joke all the time and it’s quite old.” Perhaps he was right. I never said the joke again after that (even though everyone else thought it was funny). Sometimes a good joke can run its course and it just turns to madness."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is another clue that it's Solomon. God granted Solomon with wisdom if you don't remember:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And God gave Solomon wisdom and understanding exceeding much, and largeness of heart, even as the sand that is on the sea shore.","ref":"1 Kings 4:29"},{"kind":"para","text":"Yet, he used that wisdom for personal gain. He sought to please himself first before he pleased God. Sure, he made and constructed the house of the Lord, but he lived very much apart from the Lord. His one thousand wives were mainly people from different cultures and backgrounds. They were people who served false gods. Because of this, Solomon sought pleasure in everything “under the heaven” rather than what pleasures the Lord has stored up for him in Heaven."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth and rust doth corrupt, and where thieves break through and steal: But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust doth corrupt, and where thieves do not break through nor steal: For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.","ref":"Matthew 6:19-21"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, a really good verse for Solomon to consider, yet it won’t be written until much time later, after Solomon is long gone and dead. His wisdom, however, should have shown him that, so that tells me he ignored it to seek after earthly things. He now realizes how useless that endeavor was. In fact, he gives a list:"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Looking at this list of possessions that Solomon had, you would not be able to point anything out as necessarily “sinful.” Nothing in this list is bad. These are things that rich people have even still today. Solomon never had to leave his house for want, because he had everything he needed right there. If it were available, he probably would have had a McDonald’s in his front foyer because it was possible (like Richie Rich). He had everything at his disposal and more. Nothing was out of reach. Even the best musicians stood in his presence. I am sure he enjoyed all that ability for most of his life. Yet, now that he’s come to the end of his life, he’s looking back."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"All of these works were just a breath of breaths. They were all useless now that he is old. Actually, he’s looking back at it all and wondering if he had wasted all his time getting all of this. If he had lived after his son took the throne, he would see that he certainly did waste all his time. Everything that Solomon built will be gone throughout the years to come. Even Israel will be captured and taken, and there will be no king left in Israel (paving the way for the Lord Jesus Christ to sit on His rightful throne). Solomon would certainly be crushed by the direction Israel will go. In all honesty, he would probably shoulder the blame for it, because he set them up so high that the only other direction to go in was down. I don’t place all the blame on Solomon though. Obviously, every person is individually responsible for their following and calling, and none of them will take it seriously (or even into consideration) that they should be seeking the Lord and His ways. But Solomon should have prepared the people for that."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was called into ministry, I was painted this picture of unity and joy about it that is not necessarily correct. The people I learned under always seemed so happy and excited about what God was doing in the church. Especially my pastor at the time. Everyone was moving forward for one cause and for one goal, and that was to build the church and reach the lost. After I joined ministry, I quickly realized that what you see behind the pulpit on Sundays is not what you get in the pastor’s office throughout the rest of the week. He’s joyous in front of the crowd and burdened behind them. The joy that pastors spew in their sermons is not the lifestyle they are actually living. Honestly, I think most pastors that I have met (if not all the pastors I’ve met) live a very sad and lonely lifestyle. How I wish someone would have prepared me for what real ministry is like. Ever since I’ve been in it, I try to represent (with all sincerity and consideration) what it’s really like both behind the scenes and in front of people. I’ve given warning after warning about what it was really like, and most people don’t want to believe it. I’ve had several people come to me after going into paid ministry telling me that I was right and they should have listened."},{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon should have warned his people about what was going to come. You might say, “Well, how would Solomon have known?” It’s written in God’s Word! Solomon was full of wisdom, don’t forget, and he knew everything about pretty much everything. He knew how things worked and why they needed to work that way. So he knew his scriptures no doubt, and yet he didn’t take his scriptures seriously. Moses was not as wise as Solomon, yet Moses is seen warning the people about what is to come."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But it shall come to pass, if thou wilt not hearken unto the voice of the LORD thy God, to observe to do all his commandments and his statutes which I command thee this day; that all these curses shall come upon thee, and overtake thee: Cursed shalt thou be in the city, and cursed shalt thou be in the field. Cursed shall be thy basket and thy store. Cursed shall be the fruit of thy body, and the fruit of thy land, the increase of thy kine, and the flocks of thy sheep. Cursed shalt thou be when thou comest in, and cursed shalt thou be when thou goest out. The LORD shall send upon thee cursing, vexation, and rebuke, in all that thou settest thine hand unto for to do, until thou be destroyed, and until thou perish quickly; because of the wickedness of thy doings, whereby thou hast forsaken me.","ref":"Deuteronomy 28:15-20"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems the only one holding the curse at this particular moment in Ecclesiastes is Solomon himself. These are his warnings on paper, but I don’t know what he has actually said aloud to his children. He should have warned, like Moses, the entire multitude of people. He should have read these verses to them. All these curses, and more, will come to them by the way. They face exactly what God warns them about because they turn from the Lord altogether to search after fake gods that don’t do anything for them but help them sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"Again, this is the mind of Christ as well, and I hope that is not lost here. Though it’s Solomon saying these things, Christ is minded the same way. Nothing in this Earth is worth the labor for except to get to the next day if we are doing service without the Lord. That is the key to all of this, by the way. We must labor WITH God, or the labor will be in vain."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":17,"title":"One Event","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon has experienced it all. There’s nothing that anyone else can do that would be different from what Solomon had already done. They will say it’s new (Solomon already established that), but it’s the same things."},{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon lived a life of luxury and came out on the other side saying, “None of it was really worth it. The only thing I learned was how meaningless and ill-fated that venture has been. It has led me to nothing more than regret.”"}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Well, obviously, being wise is better than not knowing anything at all. However, the world’s definition of wisdom is not what the Lord’s definition is. According to scripture, wisdom is Christ. In contrast, the world often equates wisdom with age. Yet, I’ve met quite a few older men who are not very wise. Honestly, it’s because they have rejected Christ."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What is the event that happens to both the wise and the fool? This should be a rhetorical question, but I’m afraid there are people who aren’t thinking clearly about this. The one event that happens to every human being, and the event that Solomon will be contemplating throughout this entire book, is death. We all die. It doesn’t matter how much knowledge or wisdom we accumulate; we will end up in the ground in due time. It doesn’t matter how much life we have lived; we will still end up in the same situation as a fool. The only difference between a saved man and an unsaved man is their salvation. Both, however, end up in the same ground."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon’s wisdom, despite its abundance, will not prevent him from meeting the same fate as a fool. He will die too. He could not find a way to live forever. I believe there are many “wise” people in our world today still trying to figure it out. They are creating medications and cryopods to keep their bodies alive after they have passed, hoping to preserve their old, withered bodies so they can return to life when a cure for death is discovered."},{"kind":"para","text":"Friend, I will warn you today, you don’t have to be a scholar to understand that science will never favor that conclusion. There is no cure for death. Take it from the wisest man to have ever lived, Solomon. His wisdom could not find a cure, and the world’s wisdom certainly won’t. I assume that Solomon probably really looked for a cure, but in the end, he realized that there was nothing he could do. So what’s the point? Well, there is one solution, and that is through Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.","ref":"Romans 6:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon figures this out, and he will give that answer at the end of this book, but I want to give the answer ahead of time. Perhaps you stumbled upon this commentary because you don’t know, but Jesus is the cure for death. Maybe you already knew that, and you are already saved, but it’s a good reminder to every reader that Solomon has a cure, but it was never found in his own brainpower and knowledge. It was only found in the trust and hope of the Lord, who will send His Son to die for Solomon on the cross. That was what Solomon should have been promoting his entire life. I suppose riches blinded him to what was really important until it was too late."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have no doubt that there are a lot of people throughout history who were extremely wise, far wiser than we even knew possible. Plato and Aristotle were probably very smart individuals. Stoicism probably has some wisdom to it. I’m sure there are even more people included in the faith who were smart, wise, and well-accounted for, yet we don’t hear about these people anymore today. Their ideas are outdated."},{"kind":"para","text":"Someone, somewhere, is keeping the ideas of Darwin alive (for some reason I don’t understand), and they treat him as a wise being. I’ve read the Theory of Evolution, and I don’t find any wisdom in it. Theories don’t produce wisdom. Solutions do, but there is no solution in Darwin’s theory. Either way, in due time, his theory on the matter will disappear for something or someone new, and that person will have an even more warped idea of the beginning of all things that people will try to twist into their own brains and understandings, even though there is no logical conclusion to it. That person will eventually disappear too. We move along far too quickly."},{"kind":"para","text":"I believe that everyone has ADHD to some level, and we all just forget about everything before too long. I have no doubt that even this commentary will probably disappear into the nether before too long. Not that I’m wise by any sort of degree. Honestly, most pastors would probably call me anything but wise, but I write these not for my own wisdom’s sake. I write these because I want to learn with the readers as we go through it. Hopefully, there is some wisdom poured out of this commentary, but I pray that it’s not because of any sort of wisdom that I have personally mustered up. Like Solomon, if you get any wisdom from anything I wrote, I hope it points you back to scripture and back to His finished work on the cross."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, same my man. I think people who struggle with life the most are those who have understood how vain life really is. Inventing, progressing, trying, and failing at many things is good for a short time, but after a while, it just becomes redundant and boring. I figured that out in high school. I came to the conclusion that the rest of my life would just be this mess. I would get up early, go to a place I don’t want to go, learn a few things, do a few things, talk to a few people, go home, play a game or watch TV, go to sleep, and do it all over again until the weekend, where I would have to get up and take care of chores. How boring life really is if you think about it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ gives us new life, though. We are learning a different life through Him. The people who find life boring must need a fresh Spirit, and that can only be found in a life following Jesus. While I’ve had my trials and pains following Christ, I can honestly say it has never been boring."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"It is of the LORD's mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are new every morning: great is thy faithfulness.","ref":"Lamentations 3:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s gift of mercy is “new” every morning we wake up. Nothing gets old when you are living a life following Christ. It might get tiresome or burdensome, but it does not get old."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":23,"title":"The End of Labor","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"That man being Rehoboam, his son, Solomon hated the idea that his son was going to take over his hard work. There was a lot that Solomon was leaving behind, yet he resented the notion of his son inheriting it. For me, I look forward to the day that my daughters will lead their own lives and take the things I leave behind. I, of course, have a different perspective than Solomon does. I am not a rich king who has experienced everything there is to experience in life. However, I am still leaving things behind for my children, and I am hopeful that they will use what I leave for their personal growth and that of their families. This commentary is one of those things. While the books themselves may never see real value, the information will be there for them."},{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon is leaving this book of Ecclesiastes to his children, and you would think he would look forward to his children taking over the kingdom and using his wisdom. Yet, he doesn’t like this idea at all. This is what he says about it:"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He doesn’t know if his son will do what is right. He doesn’t know if his son will be wise in his leadership. I think that was a real worry for him now that he’s coming so close to his death. I don’t know if he ever actually prepared Rehoboam for this thing."},{"kind":"para","text":"He pleads with his children in Proverbs, and now he warns his children in Ecclesiastes, and yet there is no actual instruction given to his children throughout any other part of scripture. I’m assuming he didn’t have a very strong relationship with his kids. David wasn’t a great father either. Solomon didn’t learn how to be a father from David that is for sure. Either way, Solomon realizes now that he should have started pouring into his child what was important far before all of this took place."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is old, he will not depart from it.","ref":"Proverbs 22:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"And yet Rehoboam departs from everything that Solomon does. I don’t think Solomon “raised” him up in the way he should go. I think Solomon waited until it was too late. Solomon was too busy with his own life and wants. If we are not careful, we can fall for the same traps as well. Don’t set up for your own future. If you have children, set up for THEIR future."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He worked hard for where he is today, and he is not just left with “vanity.” He’s left with a “great evil.” The more he learned, the more evil he grasped from it. Interestingly enough, that is quite the opposite position that the Lord wants for his children."},{"kind":"para","text":"In the beginning (in the garden), Adam and Eve didn’t know evil. They knew what was good because God told them what was good. Everything that God made was good. Yet, when they bit from the fruit, they knew evil. They knew evil, and it gave them more knowledge, leading them into even more evil. Good was their life before the fruit. Evil was their life after the fruit. Evil has been passed down because of it, and we all know evil. Arguably, the smarter a man is, the more evil he will experience. Perhaps knowledge is not all that it’s cracked up to be."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I know a few people who are looking forward to retirement. They have worked a good portion of their lives and are ready to call it quits. I wonder how it will be in their retirement. Do you think they will look back and say, “I’m glad I worked all those years. I’m glad I spent the most portion of my life in labor. It was all worth it now that I’m retired.”? I doubt it."},{"kind":"para","text":"They will probably look back and say, “I wish I would have taken a little more time with my family when I had the chance. I wish I would have used those vacation days instead of letting them build up. I wish I would have told my family that I loved them and shown that to them through my presence.” Even at the end of life, that is what they will look at. Everyone regrets not being more present with the people they loved because they were focused on pursuing rest for themselves first and foremost."}]}]},{"start":24,"end":26,"title":"God's Gifts","entries":[{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There’s some sense here. It’s a glimmer of hope, even if it’s for a moment. He finally talks about a gift from God’s hand. That gift was eating, drinking, and enjoying what he had worked so hard for. But this gift and glimmer of hope will vanish in a moment. Why? Is it because God’s gifts don’t last? No. It’s because our focus is not persistently on the Lord. We miss His gifts to us because we are focused on the gift itself."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’m sure you have given something to a child before as a gift. They love the gift. They will play with the gift, use the gift, and dwell over the gift. But they forget the person that gave it to them."}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"See, Solomon focused on the gift rather than the giver."}]},{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the end, the Lord is going to give to everyone what He deems worthy for that person. A sinner will be gifted from the Lord too, and those who are saved might look at the gifts of a sinner (an unrepentant one at that) and think, “Why is God good to that man?” It just makes us feel bitter against the Lord, even though the Lord gifts His own children too. We should not be focused on what the gifts are. That is the important lesson to learn from this chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"Solomon was focused on all his material possessions. He was not focused on the one who provided his riches for him. If Solomon’s sight was fixed on the Lord, these gifts would be an obvious sign of God’s provision. Instead, Solomon simply focused on the gifts and forsook the Lord who gave them, attributing his rewards to his own effort and work. If it’s through your own efforts, the rewards at the end are always going to be vanity and vexation of spirit."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":26}]},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 03"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Solomon made it clear that all the material wealth and work that he put into this life and his riches all amounted to nothing more than a \"breath of breaths.\" Everything, in the end, was vanity, and it only made him feel even more empty in this life. He had it all, and there was really no reason for him to complain, but he was still lacking something. So, he's looking for something more meaningful. Something that will keep him going and give him reason to live."},{"kind":"para","text":"This chapter, he highlights the seasons of life a man will go through while he's here. He is trying to find purpose and meaning through the hardships and the victories of everything. He wants something to make sense. But as we already know, he will not find any satisfaction in the highs and lows of life. The end result will always be the same, and he will address that in every single one of these chapters as we read. It all end the same way. Death, and vanity."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":15,"title":"A Season For Everything","entries":[]},{"start":16,"end":22,"title":"Everything Is The Same","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":22}]},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 04"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Solomon thought deeply about the different seasons that every person will face in life. He gave positive seasons and negative seasons, and they all will come in cycles. Just like the sun, the wind, and the rain, seasons of trials and victory will absolutely come to every human being. He concluded the chapter with simply being content with what you have rather than looking at the season. Sadly, that is not going to give him much hope for life either."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he comes back to reality, and realizes that there is a lot more depth to life than up and down seasons. There are feelings, and actual hurts from people that he is ruling over. He is seeing that there are people who are not happy, and people who are being mistreated, and he has to do something about that as their leader. Furthermore, he worries about what the next leader will do later on and if anyone will care about these hurt and needy people. We get to look towards the future when reading the Old Testament, and we can see that there really isn't any leaders who will make things better sadly..."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Oppression Speaks","entries":[]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":"Envy Kills","entries":[]},{"start":7,"end":12,"title":"Isolation Is Sin","entries":[]},{"start":13,"end":16,"title":"A Wise Leader","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":16}]},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 05"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previoulsy: In our last chapter, Solomon looked at authority and power as his next fixation for meaning in this life. He doesn't find it their either. He has been searching for what really matters in life and can't seem to hold onto anything. It all loses it's purpose and flow after a while. You accumulate until you have so much that it doesn't seem to even matter anymore."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Solomon will look to religion to fill the hole in his heart. He will find that religion has some satisfying things in it, but he will also see that it (too) leads to the same fate as everything else he has already looked into. I believe Solomon was a really good religious guy, but that was what ultimately led him into writing this book of the Bible."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"Religious Practice","entries":[]},{"start":8,"end":9,"title":"A Broken World","entries":[]},{"start":10,"end":12,"title":"More and Less","entries":[]},{"start":13,"end":17,"title":"What Is Value?","entries":[]},{"start":18,"end":20,"title":"Rich Against Poor","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":20}]},
    "6": {"chapter":6,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 06"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter Solomon focused on the pursuit of religion. It seemed like it would be something that would suffice for a man, and many men have gone into thinking that it would be the perfect thing to bridge the gap in their lives and hearts, but just like those men, Solomon finds that there is nothing lasting even in religion. Arguably, Solomon was the most religious man to have ever lived. He probably practiced every sort of religious guise that was available to him. Yet he found nothing valuable there after a while. It all led to the same dismal and stale spirit that he had from pursuing worldly pleasures."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, he sums up much of what he has already spoken about in the previous chapters. He looks at how depraved man really is, and how it doesn't ever seem to end in this world. He talks about how unending the work of man is, and how unfulfilling the pursuit of wealth is no matter how much of it you try to take for yourself. It will never stay with you when you die, though, and that is really what disappoints Solomon here."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Continual Evil","entries":[]},{"start":7,"end":9,"title":"Jesus Is Good","entries":[]},{"start":10,"end":12,"title":"The Benefits Of God","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":12}]},
    "7": {"chapter":7,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 07"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Solomon pointed out how man is continually evil even though there are some good things in the world. He takes a look at how meaningless our ventures are for trying to produce good in our own strength (something he talks about in nearly every chapter of this book), and the benefits of having God in your life and pursuing after His goodness and gifts."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Solomon ends up in the middle road mentality, where he doesn't want to stand affirmative on either side. He tries this mentality for a while, but finds out that it also leads to the same conclusion. Actually, I think it might end up to an even more vain lifestyle than any of these other pursuits. Not having a firm foundation is a very shaky place to put yourself in, and it usually doesn't leave you ending with anything really worthwhile except for more pain and problems."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Middle Road","entries":[]},{"start":5,"end":7,"title":"Wise Rebuke","entries":[]},{"start":8,"end":10,"title":"Which Spirit Serves?","entries":[]},{"start":11,"end":14,"title":"Leading to Christ","entries":[]},{"start":15,"end":18,"title":"Holding Onto Fear","entries":[]},{"start":19,"end":22,"title":"Wisdom Is Strength","entries":[]},{"start":23,"end":29,"title":"Wisdom Is Far","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":29}]},
    "8": {"chapter":8,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 08"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Solomon talked about what it looks like for a man to stand in the middle of the road of faith (something he will talk more about in this chapter). He talked about the necessity to listen to rebuke from wise fellowship, what Spirit we should serve with, how the road leading to Christ is worth the effort, keeping a view of fearing God, applying wisdom to increase your spiritual strength, and coming to terms with how far \"wisdom\" may seem. Though wisdom is not far. It's found in Jesus Christ who is near everyone that believes:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But what saith it? The word is nigh thee, even in thy mouth, and in thy heart: that is, the word of faith, which we preach; That if thou shalt confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and shalt believe in thine heart that God hath raised him from the dead, thou shalt be saved. For with the heart man believeth unto righteousness; and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation.","ref":"Romans 10:8-10"},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Solomon talks even deeper about a lukewarm faith. This is actually a very practical chapter on faith, that I believe every strong Christian (and especially every weak Christian) must come to terms with and work on (including myself). There is a lot of really great wisdom in this chapter that I think will lead us to the track to Christ and faith."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Time & Judgement","entries":[]},{"start":9,"end":13,"title":"Blaming The Oppressed","entries":[]},{"start":14,"end":15,"title":"Righteousness Mistreated","entries":[]},{"start":16,"end":17,"title":"Applying Your Heart","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":17}]},
    "9": {"chapter":9,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 09"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Solomon spoke about the ministry of time and judgement, the terrible work of blaming the oppressed for the problems of political leaders, the truth about how good people are treated like evil people (and the other way around), ending the chapter with what the work of God should bring (seeing as the world doesn't bring much usefulness by anything else). Every chapter brings us closer and closer to an important truth that Solomon (rather the preacher) has been (seemingly) skirting around this entire time. Our minds are too focused on the temporal things."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we get more temporal thoughts, but Solomon see's that there is a connection to better meaning because of the mistreatment of a wise man. He goes back on something he said earlier and makes a slight correction (something he will have to do again by the end of this book). There is more to life than the simple seasons of good and bad. There's more to life than what you can simply see. That is the beauty of this book of the Bible."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":1,"title":"Hate & Love","entries":[]},{"start":2,"end":3,"title":"There's One Conclusion","entries":[]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":"An End of Faith","entries":[]},{"start":7,"end":10,"title":"A Life Of Vanity","entries":[]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"Without Christ","entries":[]},{"start":13,"end":18,"title":"Wisdom Saves","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":18}]},
    "10": {"chapter":10,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 10"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: In our last chapter, Solomon showed why the world's version of love is not a lasting or useful version of it. It gets mixed up with hate because they both have the same characteristics if you are superficial about it. He talks about how \"injust\" it seems that everyone (no matter how rich, or how smart you might be) goes to the same end in life (that is death). He talks about dead things, and how seemingly meaningless life is because everything turns to vanity... that is, it's all vanity without Christ. He talked about how real wisdom can SAVE, and worldly wisdom will be buried with the wise man. He seems to be changing some of his thoughts in a few areas, as he grows to understand that this is not all that there is."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, we go back to injustices here on Earth, and look at how even the smallest actions can destroy good things. He seems to be worried about leadership specifically in this chapter, and I think this is a great chapter for leaders to really study and follow because there are far too many leaders who have forsaken the things that Solomon warns about here."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":7,"title":"No Justice","entries":[]},{"start":8,"end":11,"title":"Sharpening Tools","entries":[]},{"start":12,"end":15,"title":"Burdened Leaders","entries":[]},{"start":16,"end":20,"title":"Leaders Eat Last","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":20}]},
    "11": {"chapter":11,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 11"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Solomon has done a good job since the beginning of this book at pointing out how meaningless most of life's pursuits are. We spend years upon years in this world trying to scratch an itch and find the next best thing, when in reality there is no end to that race until death. Even if you finish the race of that longing, you will go to the grave empty. We look (in the last chapter) at some small change in the demeanor of the preacher. He is realizing that there are good and fine things in this life amidst the vanity, and it's not found in temporal or physical objects. The good things in this life are from above first. That will make more sense in chapter twelve. The things that Solomon spoke about in our last chapter were extremely valuable for leadership today to examine themselves with."},{"kind":"para","text":"In this chapter, Solomon hits some more very good points about sowing the best you can today, because there will be a time in the future where you will have to reap from it. What you say and do today will collect interest over time, and it's really up to you for what you will hope to collect in the future."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":8,"title":"Right Now","entries":[]},{"start":9,"end":10,"title":"Stay Young","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":10}]},
    "12": {"chapter":12,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"Chapter: Chapter 12"},{"kind":"para","text":"Status: In progress"},{"kind":"para","text":"## Previously: Solomon has spent every single chapter of this book looking at the life of vanity. It's the life of a normal person honestly. You probably fall into one of these chapters somewhere. If you fall into more than one chapter you have certainly been blessed to experience life. However, none of that leads to anything. It's a deadend road. There is a rewarding life to live, but it's not found in any of those themes spoken about in this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"Rather, in this chapter, we will see what real genuine life is made of. We should not be seeking to please ourselves or the world. We should only be looking to please the Lord, and Solomon will finally culminate all of his learning into the most needful lifestyle a man can have, and that is in seeking out Christ and sharing Him with others."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":10,"title":"Looking Up","entries":[]},{"start":11,"end":12,"title":"Live In Wisdom","entries":[]},{"start":13,"end":14,"title":"The Conclusion","entries":[]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":14}]},
  },
};
